        "message": [
          "%md",
          "### Make Relationships Easy to Review",
          "",
          "Bob compared ranked network evidence, inspected creator 1's outgoing connections, and examined the creators promoting brand 1. The table and map in each focused exercise describe the same recorded relationships: the table makes properties easy to compare, and the map makes direction and shared context easy to follow.",
          "",
          "Graph layout and node positions can vary. Compare stored IDs, handles, brand names, and edge properties when checking the result.",
          "",
          "Bob hands the relationship evidence to Moon Kai, the spatial specialist. The next question for Seer Sporting Goods is where to review fulfillment options for the products receiving attention.",
          "",
          "The optional section below demonstrates a separate in-memory workflow. It is not required for either SQL evidence-to-map comparison."
        ],
        "result": null,
        "visualizationConfig": null
      },
      {
        "row": 0,
        "col": 0,
        "sizeX": 0,
        "width": 12,
        "title": "Optional: the separate in-memory graph workflow",
        "hasTitle": true,
        "selectedVisualization": "html",
        "hideCode": true,
        "hideResult": false,
        "hideGutter": true,
        "hideVizConfig": false,
        "hideInIFrame": false,
        "enabled": true,
        "forms": "[]",
        "relations": [],
        "dynamicFormParams": "{}",
        "message": [
          "%md",
          "The earlier %sql paragraphs query the database SQL property graph. This optional section loads that graph into the PGX server and uses %pgql-pgx. The SQL graph source type is pg_sql, not pg_pgql. PGQL uses FROM MATCH ... ON graph and LIMIT; it is not the SQL Worksheet syntax. Run the Python load before the PGQL query. The in-memory graph is a loaded snapshot; rerun the SQL paragraphs for current database evidence.",
          "",
          "Oracle documentation: https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/paas/autonomous-database/csgru/load-graphs-memory-programmatically.html"
        ],
        "result": null,
        "visualizationConfig": null
      },
      {
        "row": 0,
        "col": 0,
        "sizeX": 0,
        "width": 12,
        "title": "Load the SQL property graph into PGX",
        "hasTitle": true,
        "selectedVisualization": "raw",
        "hideCode": false,
        "hideResult": false,
        "hideGutter": true,
        "hideVizConfig": false,
        "hideInIFrame": false,
        "enabled": true,
        "forms": "[]",
        "relations": [],
        "dynamicFormParams": "{}",
        "message": [
          "%python-pgx",
          "GRAPH_NAME = \"INFLUENCER_NETWORK\"",
          "GRAPH_OWNER = \"LLUSER\"",
          "graph = session.read_graph_by_name(GRAPH_NAME, \"pg_sql\", schema=GRAPH_OWNER)",
          "print(\"Loaded graph:\", graph.name)"
        ],
        "result": null,
        "visualizationConfig": null
      },
